WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.660
Sockets are one of the most direct ways a C program communicates beyond its own address space.

2
00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:17.880
A socket can connect processes on the same host, clients across a network, services inside a container platform, or components that run under different user accounts and privilege levels.

3
00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:23.740
That makes a socket more than a transport detail; it is a boundary where outside data meets internal state.

4
00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:28.340
The peer on a socket may have a very different level of trust from the receiving process.

5
00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:37.960
It may be a user tool, another service, an internal component, a health checker, a client from a private network, or a process running in a different container.

6
00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:45.020
The code should not treat the connection as trusted simply because it is local, internal, or expected during normal operation.

7
00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:49.140
Secure socket programming begins with explicit assumptions.

8
00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:50.360
Who may connect?

9
00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:52.860
Which interface should be reachable?

10
00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:54.560
Which messages are valid?

11
00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:56.900
Which requests require authorization?

12
00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:58.680
How much data is acceptable?

13
00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:04.060
What should happen when the peer disconnects, sends incomplete data, or keeps a connection idle?

14
00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:08.720
These assumptions need to be visible in the design and enforced in code.

15
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:14.840
Socket defects can affect confidentiality, integrity, availability, and isolation.

16
00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:26.380
A service may disclose data to the wrong peer, perform an unintended action, corrupt state by accepting malformed input, or become unstable because the protocol has no limits.

17
00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:31.360
These failures are often the result of ordinary omissions rather than dramatic mistakes.

18
00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:36.280
A defensive socket design treats every socket as a trust boundary.

19
00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:45.500
Endpoint exposure, peer trust, protocol parsing, resource ownership, timeouts, transport protection, and cleanup are all part of the same story.

20
00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:49.760
The goal is predictable behavior when the network and peers are imperfect.
